---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';
import { GRADES, LEVELS } from '../lib/tools/gpa';
const canonical = '/methodology';
const title = `How We Calculate | ${SITE.name}`;
const description = 'Every formula, every default, and everything each model deliberately leaves out.';
---
<BaseLayout {title} {description} {canonical}>
  <div class="page-band">
    <div class="page">
      <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Methodology</span>
      </nav>
      <div class="tool-hero">
        <h1>How we calculate</h1>
        <p>The formulas, the defaults, and the limits — including the one thing this site cannot check for you.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="page">
    <section class="prose" style="margin-top:var(--s-6)">
      <h2>Where the numbers come from</h2>
      <p>
        Almost uniquely in this portfolio, <strong>this hub publishes no
        external data</strong>. There is no rate table to verify, no statutory
        limit, no government figure. Every number on every page comes from what
        you type. That is a property of the subject, not a gap in the research.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two conventions the site does supply, both worth stating plainly
        because neither is a fact.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The grade scale.</strong> {GRADES.length} grades from A+ to F on
        the standard four-point scale. A+ counts 4.0, not 4.3 — most US
        institutions cap the unweighted scale at 4.0, and the higher value
        would inflate every GPA in the direction nobody double-checks.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The weighting.</strong> {LEVELS.map((l) => `${l.label} +${l.bonus.toFixed(1)}`).join(', ')}.
        The most common convention, and not a standard. Schools weight
        differently, cap how many weighted courses count, or run a 5.0 scale
        throughout. Where our figure and your school's disagree, your school is
        right, and every GPA page says so.
      </p>

      <h2>GPA</h2>
      <p>
        Credit-weighted average of grade points: each course's points times its
        credits, summed, divided by total credits. Weighted GPA adds the level
        bonus to each course's points before the same calculation, which is why
        it routinely exceeds 4.0.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Excluded deliberately:</strong> deciding which courses count.
        Colleges recalculating a GPA often drop electives and count only core
        academic subjects, and that judgement is local. Pass/fail courses,
        withdrawals and repeats are left to you for the same reason.
      </p>

      <h2>Grade needed on the final</h2>
      <p>
        Each graded component contributes its score times its weight to a
        100-point course total. Subtract what is banked from the target, divide
        by the weight still ungraded: that is the mark the final must carry.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two results are reported as outcomes rather than numbers. Above 100%
        means the target is unreachable; at or below zero means the grade is
        already secured. Printing "you need 118%" is correct and useless.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Excluded deliberately:</strong> dropped lowest scores, curves,
        scaling and rounding. All are local, all change the answer, none can be
        known from here — so the figure is the mark needed before any of them,
        which is the conservative direction.
      </p>

      <h2>Student loan payoff</h2>
      <p>
        Month by month: interest accrues on each loan at one twelfth of its
        annual rate, minimums apply, then any extra goes to one target loan
        chosen by strategy. A cleared loan releases its minimum into the next
        month's attack — modelled for all three strategies, not only the one
        named after it.
      </p>
      <p>
        All three are computed every time, so the comparison between them is
        real rather than asserted.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Excluded deliberately:</strong> interest capitalisation events,
        servicer allocation rules, variable rates, deferment, forbearance and
        income-driven repayment. The last matters most — if you are working
        toward forgiveness, paying extra can actively cost you money, and this
        model cannot know that.
      </p>

      <h2>What none of these are</h2>
      <p>
        Not advice, academic or financial. A GPA here is an estimate; your
        institution's calculation is the one that counts. A payoff schedule
        depends on terms you entered and a servicer behaving as assumed.
        Confirm anything you plan to act on.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
